/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape02_water_gas_shift
  ===========================

  THE SHIFT LANDSCAPE -- equimolar CO + H2O (gibbs01_water_gas_shift's own
  feed and species set), the fraction of the feed carbon leaving as CO2
  against temperature at 1, 10 and 100 bar.  `elementYield` on carbon with
  CO2 as the product IS the CO conversion here, because every carbon atom
  fed arrives as CO -- and it is the forum-permitted metric (species
  "conversion" is banned where the species is also produced;
  docs/design/gibbs-map-forum-2026-07-02.md).

  WHAT THE THREE CURVES SHOW, and it is one curve.  Exothermic (the
  conversion falls with T) and mole-CONSERVING (2 -> 2), so the three
  pressures give the SAME equilibrium to every printed digit: the ideal-gas
  equilibrium constant of a Delta_n = 0 reaction carries no P.  The page
  draws the three curves on top of each other and says why.  MEASURED, not
  assumed: no cell on this grid condenses -- the three pressures agree to
  every printed digit at all 46 temperatures, which they could not if water
  had left the gas anywhere (at 400 K the equilibrium leaves 2.5 % water in
  the gas, 2.5 bar at 100 bar against a 2.46 bar Psat -- and the run's own
  identity of the three columns is the proof, not this estimate).

  WHY THE PRESSURES ARE 1, 10 AND 100 BAR.  `Pgrid` is `from / to / n / log`
  (GibbsMapOp.cpp reads no list form), and three log-spaced points from 1 to
  100 bar land on the decades a textbook draws.  Ideal-gas validity is the
  same caveat as nh3_equilibrium_map's: the high-pressure curve is the
  SHAPE of the pressure dependence, not a quantitative prediction.

  temperatureApproach IS DECLARED, AT ZERO.  The EduTool's slider replaces
  the NUMBER of a declared scalar and can add no key (gui/src/case/
  methodRun.ts), so the key must be here for the page to move it.  Zero is
  true equilibrium and prints nothing; a magnitude > 0 makes the run assign
  the sign at EVERY cell from the thermicity of the overall transformation
  at that cell's T (GibbsMapOp.cpp, `resolveDirection`) and print the tally.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      wgs;
        type      gibbsMap;
        elements  ( C H O );
        species
        (
            { name CO;    atoms ( 1 0 1 ); }
            { name water; atoms ( 0 2 1 ); }
            { name CO2;   atoms ( 1 0 2 ); }
            { name H2;    atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
        );
        feed      { CO 1.0;  water 1.0; }
        Tgrid     { from 400; to 1300; n 46; }             // K
        Pgrid     { from 1.0e5; to 1.0e7; n 3; log true; }  // Pa: 1, 10, 100 bar
        temperatureApproach 0;                          // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        metric    { type elementYield; element C; product CO2; }
        anchors
        (
            { T 800;      P 1.0e5;   }   // gibbs01's operating point
        );
        output    { file map.csv; }
    }
);
